<p>Review below</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>[All types of SCP-3287 are nearly identical in chemical composition, but are distinguished by unique anomalous compounds that supposedly alter their effects.]<br />
This sentence is contradicting itself. &quot;Nearly identical in chemical composition&quot; means that the individual molecules in can 1 are the all the same, but maybe have an extra carbon or a nitrogen instead of a carbon compared to the molecules in can 2, while &quot;distinguished by unique anomalous compounds&quot; means that most of the molecules in can 1 and can 2 are the same but there's a little bit of different anomalous molecules added in each. Basically the first means the perfume bottles are ethyl alcohol and isopropyl alcohol respectively, while the second means that they're both ethyl alcohol but the first has a little bit of lemon and the second has orange. I think you're intending the first one, to rank them under SCP-3287 and [Despite being similar in composition to SCP-3287-1]. To do that you could say something like [Differences in chemical composition alter their anomalous effects.], sharpened up by your writing.</p>
<p>[This has been used to artificially preserve certain species of animals in the wild by sterilizing their predators]<br />
&#8230;and driving their predators to extinction instead. Maybe better to say that we used them to sterilize human predators, or preserve species that make valuable products like that amnestic-making cactus, instead of something that's going to fuck with the ecosystem whichever way you slice it.</p>
<p>[and also the type with the most documented information]<br />
Passive voice sounds weird and leads to <em>wait, what?</em> moments like this. Try [the type that is the most researched] instead of [most documented information], Foundation is the one doing the documenting of the information. It's still passive voice but more clear.</p>
<p>[SCP-3287-3 is the third type of SCP-3287. There is no documentation of any attempted tests with SCP-3287-3.]<br />
Except maybe that's what you intended, for the antimemetic/Foundation to be unsure if they've actually done that research or not. It's still kind of clumsy, given that Foundation is also referring to the fact that they have done more logged tests with SCP-3287-1 than logged tests with SCP-3287-2 rather than just they've done a lot of tests with SCP-3287-1 and some number that is eating itself with SCP-3287-3.</p>
<p>[An endeavour to only select test candidates who are not sterile has not been attempted.]<br />
Haha, I can imagine a professor shaking their head at those darn interns picking out steriles to defertilize.</p>
<p>I like the idea of memory removal (forgetting that the kids exist? removing the kids from existence?) -3, - was a nice lead up to it. The length of this makes it short and sweet. I had deja vu with -2 to unicorn horns, but that might be a nice thing to have -2 <em>be</em> unicorn horns (retroactive sterilization and death of offspring) with Foundation finding it as convergent evolution, to lead up to -3 causing offspring to be forgotten and uh something specific and awful with ΩK. I'm still not really sure what it does that differs it from -2, and while I like the ending and ending line a lot I think I missed what makes it special.</p>
